  It slips back down the beach in glassy sheets,
  unwinding every line it drew at dawn,
  and leaves behind its small and shining feats:
  a shell, a ribbon of kelp, a feather's down.

  I think of how our own lives come and go,
  the swell of joy, the ebb of what we had,
  the way that grief recedes, then comes to show
  its face again when evenings turn to sad.
  But always, past the lowest, driest sand,
  the water gathers somewhere out of sight,
  and some slow pull we cannot understand
  is turning, turning, toward the shore tonight.

  So walk the flats and gather what remains,
  and do not mourn the distance of the sea.
  It keeps an old appointment, and it deigns
  to come again, as surely as can be.
  The shore is never lost, only left dry
  a little while, to listen, and to wait
  beneath the same unhurried, patient sky
  for water's slow and faithful arithmetic.

